---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ConversionDiagram from '../../research/ConversionDiagram.astro';
import VerificationChart from '../../research/VerificationChart.astro';
import ProfileDensity from '../../research/ProfileDensity.astro';
import ProfileSpace from '../../research/ProfileSpace.astro';
---

<Base title="Research" description="Converting filament profiles between slicer dialects, through an intermediate representation made of physical quantities.">
  <article class="wrap page">
    <p class="eyebrow">Research</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Converting filament profiles between slicer dialects.</h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Bambu Lab, Prusa, Polymaker and OrcaSlicer each publish filament settings in their own format — their own dialect.
        I'm studying whether these can be converted systematically into settings for a different machine.
        As far as I could find, there is no prior work on this problem.
      </p>

      <h2>Method.</h2>
      <p>
        I analysed more than 7,000 published profiles (four sources, as of July 2026). The conversion runs in two stages: the source dialect is first
        rewritten as a physical intermediate representation (volumetric flow, thermal history, bead geometry, cooling rate),
        and the target dialect is written out from that. Parameters are treated in three classes: material-specific,
        machine-specific, and the interaction between the two.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="figure">
      <ConversionDiagram lang="en" />
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Findings.</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>For the same filament, the official maximum volumetric flow differs by up to 2.5× between ecosystems.</li>
        <li>Nozzle temperature agrees across dialects (220 °C: 20 of 34 Prusa profiles, 20 of 21 Bambu).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="figure">
      <ProfileDensity lang="en" />
    </div>

    <div class="figure" lang="ja">
      <ProfileSpace />
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Hardware test (September 2026, PLA).</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="figure">
      <VerificationChart lang="en" />
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        More than 80% of the time difference came from different cooling thresholds. Quality was about the same, except on
        overhangs, where the existing profile did better. The cause was values that assume an auxiliary fan being copied
        across as they were. I changed the approach to treat fan values as an interaction term that depends on the machine.
      </p>

      <h2>Presentation and next steps.</h2>
      <p>I gave an oral presentation at Ginnan Gakkai 2026. Testing continues with ASA, PETG and PPS-GF20.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>


  .prose + .figure,
  .figure + .prose {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }

  .figure {
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  .figure:empty {
    display: none;
  }
</style>
